      "writtenOn": "2018-07-26T17:10:11Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I see one potential problem because of POWER_G3S5 being handled here. So the way the power sequencing works is:\n\n1. Power sequencing always starts in chipset_task in power/common.c: https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/ec/+/master/power/common.c#439\n\n2. Consider we are in G3. Loop in chipset task calls power_handle_state with state \u003d G3. No special action taken within this function. It calls common_intel_x86_power_handle_state at the bottom on line 129 with state \u003d G3.\n\n3. common_intel_x86_power_handle_state takes no action on that and control goes back to chipset_task.\n\n4. chipset_task calls power_common_state next which decides if it should transition to G3S5 based on want_g3_exit. Consider this is true and state is now set to G3S5.\n\n5. Next iteration of loop chipset_task calls power_handle_state with state \u003d G3S5. This function will set PP3300_A to 1 and start bringing the AP up i.e. wait for IN_PGOOD_ALL_CORE and then set DSWPWROK and wait for SLP_SUS. Once that is done it will pass control over to common_intel_x86_power_handle_state.\n\n6. common_intel_x86_power_handle_state now checks a number of things to decide if it is okay to boot the AP up : https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/ec/+/master/power/intel_x86.c#273 by checking battery state of charge and charger state. If it finds that something is not right, then it returns to G3.\n\nThe problem I see here is the order of operations in #5 and #6 above. Shouldn\u0027t we wait until common_intel_x86_power_handle_state decides that it is okay to boot the AP up to enable PP3300_A? There is a chipset_pre_init_callback() present in that function which allows enabling of PP3300_A at the right time: https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/ec/+/master/power/intel_x86.c#315\n\nI know this code is very similar to cannonlake but I fear that we might run into issues with low battery where the AP/whole system could just brown because PP3300_A was enabled too early.\n\nYou can do that as a follow-up, but I think we should fix this behavior. Also, that way you won\u0027t have to wait for SLP_SUS. Intel common code already handles that after calling chipset_pre_init_callback by waiting for CHIPSET_G3S5_POWERUP_SIGNAL.",
